SYDNEY COHEN
By Barry Shay
“I fanned the Babe once on an inside high pitch that would have been called a ball.  Apparently that made Ruth mad; the next time up I tried to keep the ball under that paunch he was getting around the middle. It didn’t go quite as low as I wanted it. I can still see it[s] disappearing like an aspirin tablet over the right field wall,” recalled Washington Senators pitcher Syd Cohen when he pitched against Babe Ruth. This was the day that forever associated Syd a place in baseball history as the last pitcher to allow a home run and strikeout the Babe in a Yankee uniform.  

Syd’s career in baseball span over 20 years, which included three seasons with the Washington Senators – 1934, 1936-1937 – and played and managed numerous minor league teams until 1957.   He continued to give of his time coaching local sandlots teams in El Paso, playing on old-timers or benefit teams and serving as pitching coach for Texas Western College, now  University of Texas at El Paso, aka UTEP.    

Syd Cohen was born in Baltimore and lived just outside of Carroll Park with his older brother Andy Cohen. His parents were Jewish immigrants, Manus from Russia and was earning a living as a cigar maker in Baltimore.  While Lena was listed as a stay-at-home mom.  Manus in his youth played local amateur baseball teams -- Baltimore Israelites and Baltimore West Ends. He was also given a try out for the Baltimore Orioles when the team was managed by Hughie Jennings.   

Soon after Syd was born the Census (1910) showed that the lived in the Norfolk,VA area where his younger sister Eva was born. Manus supported the family operating a grocery store.  Lena had health issues and soon moved with her children to El Paso, a border town of Juarez, Mexico, around the start of the Mexican revolution.  His parents separated during this time and Manus moved back to Baltimore. 

“Syd never married, but he had many nephews and nieces and they all loved him,” said Heidi Keller, one of Syd’s nieces and his funeral.   

Young Syd developed his baseball skills playing in sandlot teams in El Paso’s Boyland League showing his versatility playing third base, catcher, right field, and pitcher.  Describing the grammar school league, the El Paso Herald mentioned that Cohen's "has some curves which would make bigger boys jealous."   A left-handed thrower and a switch-hitter, Syd eventually grew to 5-foot-11, 180 pounds. 

At El Paso High School, Syd lettered in baseball and basketball, captaining the former during his junior and senior years. Earned the captain role as a pitcher but also as a first basemen and outfielder.  Junior year Cohen posted an errorless 1.000 fielding average as an outfielder-first baseman while batting .650. He hit .350 the following spring.     The Fort Smith (Arkansas) Twins, a Class C Western Association affiliate of St. Louis Cardinals, offered Syd a contract which he declined because his mother wanted him to finish high school.   Meanwhile on the hardwood, Cohen was named to the All-Texas basketball team following an appearance in the state finals his final two years in high school.      

During summers while in high school and college Cohen played in the Copper and Frontier Leagues until the leagues disbanded in 1927. Both circuits included players banished in the Black Sox Scandal. As a 16-year-old, he joined Fort Bayard Burros and impressed Lubbock manager Sleigh (Sled) Allen with his pitching.     In 1923, Cohen returned to Fort Bayard, playing first base.   The following summer, Syd and older brother Andy played for Hurley Miners. 

After graduating high school in the summer of 1925 played for El Paso Colts, in the Frontier League (only existed in 1925).  Hal Chase called Cohen the “finest first base he had ever seen”, the two played against each other in the Copper and Frontier Leagues.         He left the team to continue his education at Southern Methodist University in Dallas for a semester and then transferred to University of Alabama in spring semester where he earned a spot on basketball and baseball teams.   While in college he was primarily stationed at first base, In 1927 he registered two wins in pitching appearances against Mississippi State and Georgia Tech. 
 
During 1926 summer break from University of Alabama Syd played under manager Roy “Hardrock” Johnson for the Fort Bayard Veterans in the Cooper League, that won the pennant that year.  Upon joining the team Hardrock praised Syd saying: “He’s an athlete at heart and the easiest man to handle that I have ever come in contact with.”    
  
Syd would play for Hardrock later when he managed Bisbee Bees in the Arizona State League between 1928-1930 and El Paso Evening Post wrote that both had formed a close friendship.  

The final year of the Copper league Syd played for the El Paso Giants. Later he was requested to report to the Polo Grounds for a tryout with New York Giants.  He was not extended a contract, but following the advice of John McGraw he signed with Wichita Falls Sudders of the Texas League to get more experience.     Then in December Cohen signed what he considered his biggest contract with the San Francisco Seals of the Pacific Coast League (PCL).  

Prior to attending spring camp, El Paso fans presented him a Rogers Hornsby glove.  But Syd got little chance to use it;    Prior to opening day, Cohen contracted influenza and never appeared in a regular season game for the Seals. He was kept on the reserve team until his release in May.     After recovering, Syd quickly signed with his old friend Hardrock to play first base a with Bisbee.  

In 1929 Cohen split time playing between two Arizona League (ASL) teams, the Phoenix Salons and Bisbee.  Combined, he hit a personal best six home runs and was recognized by The Sporting News as the second-best fielding outfielder in the league.       Following year, he also played with Bisbee and was good enough to attract the attention of the Los Angeles Angles of the PCL and St. Louis Cardinals according to Roy, who turned them both down.  

When ASL merged with Arizona Texas League (AZ-TX) in 1931, Syd ended up playing first base for the Nogales (Mexico) Internationals. The swarthy bilingual Cohen took the name “Pablo Garcia” to appease an important team sponsor who wanted Mexican players on the roster. While earning his spot on the team, but also with the vocal fans, playing stellar defense at first base hitting batting .323.   The local Mexican baseball fans at the time had a reputation of showing their displeasure at the local games.  

Cohen was signed by Galveston Buccaneers, of Texas League in 1932, only to be released prior to opening day.  He was quickly signed as a first baseman with the El Paso Texans of the Arizona-Texas League.      In 98 games he batted .302, with 26 extra-base hits.  Cohen completed the season with a brief stint with the PCL Oakland Oaks, hitting (4-for-30) .133 in eight contests.  After Oakland he joined with the Arizona Grillers, a local city league team, where he temporary assumes the manager role while team manager Tiller “Pug”Cavet who was recovering from an eye injury.     

The Great Depression caused the AZ-TX League fold in 1932; Syd joined the PCL Portland Beavers as a pitcher.   His stay with the team was short and was where he returned to El Paso and finished out the summer playing for local Texas semipro clubs. Late in the year Cohen received another chance in the long-season PCL, engaged as a relief pitcher by the Hollywood Stars. Combined with his Portland numbers, he posted an unimpressive 1-3 record overall, but batted (9-for-26) .346. 

In 1934 Cohen joined the Minneapolis Millers of the Class AA American Association as a pitcher but was used in the outfield and first base as needed.  Allowing for a Cohen family reunion, enabling him to play with his brother Andy, the Millers second baseman.    That September, Syd was traded to the Chattanooga Lookouts of the Class A Southern Association, an affiliate of the Washington Senators.   Two weeks leading prior to being called up to the Senators, Syd filled in for an injured Mule Shirley at first base, when not pitching.

Syd Cohen made his major league debut on September 18, 1934 as an outfielder in the second game of a doubleheader in Cleveland, replacing Heine Manush who needed a rest. Batting lefty against Indians reliever Ralph Winegarner,  Cohen singled in his second at at-bat, driving in a run. Otherwise went hitless batting from both sides of the plate but played errorless left field defense in a 9-6 loss.   Two days later, Syd made his pitching debut throwing a complete game but suffering a 6-1 defeat to Cleveland.   

At the end the Senators season, they faced the Yankees at Griffth Stadium.  Al Schacht was the acting manager for the final games of the season. while Joe Cronin celebrated his honeymoon, he announced that Syd would pitch the final game of the season that would have coincided with DC’s big send off for the Babe. 

On September 29, 1934, Syd filled in for Reese Diggs who was late for his start against the Yankees, Diggs would pitch the final game of the season.  This was the history making game where Syd was the pitcher of record for Babe Ruth's last home run and strikeout in a Yankee uniform.  Cohen went the distance, spacing 11 hits and four walks well enough to emerge with an 8-5 victory. Later in life Cohen reflected back on the game saying:
“I was awed when I watched those great sluggers slam ball after ball over the fence in batting practice.  It scared me just to look at the Babe, whom I had never seen before. But I was secretly glad I was going to pitch against him.”  

Following year Syd was invited to Spring training under new manager Bucky Harris, who hired Syd’s Portland manager Spencer Abbott as one of his coaches.  Cohen was returned to Chattanooga for the 1935 season, going 16-16, with a 4.73 ERA in 270 innings pitched, the most of his pro career.  He began the ensuing season back with the Lookouts, winning a game under electric lights that set a minor league attendance record of 24,639, and sponsored a raffle for a house to a lucky fan in the stadium.     

Recalled by the Senators in June, Syd faced Lou Gehrig seven times in 1936, fanning him five times and getting him to pop up twice.   One of the games was Lou’s milestone 1800 consecutive game.  Syd replaced Jimmie DeShong in the sixth inning to face Gehrig in the 9th inning who pop out. Unfortunately for Syd the next batter, Bill Dicky earned a walk off homer giving the Yankees a 6-5 win.    In 1951 Syd stated: “One of the nicest things anybody ever wrote about me appeared in the Washington Post when Shirley Povich said, ‘If all the batters in the American League were named Lou Gehrig, Syd Cohen would be the best pitcher in the American League.’ It was true that Gehrig wasn’t too tough for me.”   

Syd would earn his first save August 17 against the Yankees, replacing Earl Whitehall in the 8th inning severing a pitch in the 9th inning that Joe DiMaggio  connected with to make the ball fly 425 feet over the center field bleachers at Griffith Stadium while Gehrig pop out to end the game. 

Early in the year with the Chattanooga he faced Roy Weatherly breaking his arm and later faced him as a Cleveland Indian when he joined with Pete Appleton to end Roy’s 20 game hitting streak.   

August 1, 1936, against the Tigers, Syd faced Marv Owen twice.  Owen got the better of him the first at bat earning a RBI.  The second time, after Al Simmons homered, Owen was hit by a pitch and had considering rushing the mound, but cooler heads prevailed.  Later Cohen and Owen would be teammates playing for Portland Beavers during World War II.  

Cohen completed his audition by going 0-2 with an inflated 5.25 ERA in 36 innings pitched for Washington that season.

To prepare for the 1937 season, Cohen played on various El Paso sandlot baseball clubs practicing to throw the screwball.    During Senators spring training he showed off his screwball, stating that “I have confidence in that pitch.”       He also began throwing a submarine curve.

Used exclusively in relief – his one start was against Cleavland Indians which was rained out after four innings with Washington ahead, 4-0.      

Cohen singled in the seventh inning in a June 18 game off young Bob Feller , who appeared in the game as a relieve pitcher for Carl Fisher.   At the end of the season, the sale of Cohen’s contract to the International League Baltimore Orioles brought his brief major league career to a close. Over parts of three Washington campaigns, he posted a 3-7 (.300) record, with a 4.54 ERA in 109 innings thrown. Over that span, he surrendered 133 base hits and 37 walks while striking out 49. With the stick, Cohen batted a meek (5-for-32) .152 but fielded his position capably with a .980 FA in his 55 mound appearances. 
 
Cohen final MLB game was a win against the Philadelphia Athletics.  He replaced John Stone who was pinch hitter for Ed Linke in the 5th.  He also singled in the 9th.  The last person to hit a homerun off Cohen was Wally Moses in the 8th inning.   Cohen would end his first full season with the Senators 2-4, with a commendable 3.11 ERA in 55 innings before being sold to the Baltimore Orioles.

He continues playing for various teams in the minor league.  In 1941 he temporarily left baseball, rejecting Rogers Hornsby, Fort Worth manager, salary of $75 a week in 1941, to start a new career in baseball as an umpire. He attended Bill McGowan’s, American League umpire, school 
in Washington, DC, earning an umpire role in North Carolina State League.   
With United States involvement in World War II Syd signed with the Portland Beavers in the PCL and played with the team until 1946.  Both times the Army draft board called upon Syd he was rejected due to high blood pressure. Prior to leaving on train to El Paso for his second Army draft request, he had 25 scoreless innings.      Upon returning to the Portland in 1943, writer Wilber Bentley of the El Paso Times shares that Cohen was perfect fielding all 57 balls, leading all pitchers in fielding.  

Thereafter, Syd posted double-digit wins totals and complete games for the  Double-A PCL Portland Beavers for four-consecutive seasons (1942-1945).   Marv Owen takes the helm as Portland manger in 1944 and looks to Syd as “my automatic Friday pitcher, who will give us a win,” — Baseball-Reference showed 12 complete games and 3 shutouts.   April 15, 1944, Syd had a special day in a double header game against the San Fransico Seals, losing the first game as a starter and winning the second game in relief.    With Marv’s injured ankle in 1945, Syd was temporary manager, the year the team won PCL pennant. During Marv’s tenure he used Cohen in a coaching role, when not pitching.       

After playing for Portland Syd was able to transition to manager player roles.  Before ending his playing career in 1957 at age 46. Cohen assumed the role as manager player guiding clubs in the Arizona-Texas League (1947, 1949-1950, 1952) and Southwest International League (1951). He Ciudad Juarez (Mexico) Indios to the AZ-TX League crown. After his Juarez role he would take the field if needed, mainly focusing on managing and developing his players skills.   During his time as manager, he entertained the spectators charging onto the field to do his “Mexican hat dance” around the umpires while exchanging words about the umpire’s decision.  Up until 1953 he had been ejected 42 times.     

The management role revealed Syd’s talent developing players.   In 1954, Don Green, a Brooklyn scout when the Bisbee team was affiliated with the Dodgers, said Syd “had to take what nobody else wanted.  They looked terrible as players.  But darn it if didn’t take the bunch and do better with it than the other managers with the more selected players.”   

In 1947 was able to transition to role of manager player he recorded his finest season as a pitcher (17-5, .773) for the El Paso Texans when the Arizona-Texas League returned after it disbanded during WWII. He was inspirational getting the Boston Red Sox to be affiliated with the team a week into the season.  El Paso Hearld writer Bob Ingram described Syd’s array of pitches that he presented to the opposing players: “Manager-Pitcher Syd Cohen was out on the mound dripping sliders, knuckles and bloopers and otherwise tantalizing the Juarez batsman half to death with his slow stuff. “    

During the 1948, Cohen was business manager for El Paso Texans.  He organized an on-field wedding for outfielder, Enrique (Bacatete) Fernandez, which ended at home plate with the couple exchanging vows after Fernandez circled the bases.      The following year he returned as manager/player when the Boston Red Sox dropped its affiliation with the team.  The “ageless” – now 43 years old Cohen pitched a complete game opening day game in 1949, 5-2.  One of the impressive games that year is when he nominated himself to start a game to record a shutout against the Miami-Globe Browns 12-0, allowing only one hit.   The team also captured the Arizona-Texas League postseason Governors Cup.  

During this time managing Bisbee (1951-53) he converted Earl Wilson from catcher to pitcher.   The following year when he returned to manage the El Paso Texans and he traded for Wilson to further mentor him as a pitcher.       

Midway through the 1955 baseball season Syd was promoted to business manager of the El Paso Texans, now in the Class B West Texas-New Mexico League. Returned to the manager’s post when skipper Jim Mangan left the team in mid-August.   The Spanish-fluent Cohen then spent the next two seasons managing the Ciudad Juarez club of the Central Mexican League.      

One anecdote about Syd’s desire to win was relayed by writer Gayle Talbot in 1953 were Bill (Buck) Elliot, at the time manager of Bisbee-Douglas Copper Kings in AZ-TX, recalls a of story of a game in 1949 were he returned to the field after rain stopped the game and a lighting bolt hit a transformer causing all lights to go out.  Syd was pitching for the El Paso Texans and leading the game 9-0. Cohen was one strike away from earning a win, facing the last man in the fifth with two strikes when the rain stopped the game.    

Bill returned to the field half hour after game ended and heard a “slush-slush” sound and noticed Syd standing on the field.  

“He had been standing out there on that mound for 30 minutes, waiting to make that last pitch. I heard later he was so wet that the tobacco in his upper pocket had dissolved and run all over him, “We nearly had it out at home  plate the next night. Syd called me a lot of things you couldn't put in print and accused me of turning out the lights on him. He won't believe it was an act of Providence yet.”

Syd was also known for volunteering his time to help others with charitable baseball causes. Between 1950 and 1954 Cohen helped assemble and manage a local all-star team to play a charity game organized by Art Nehf and the New York Giants, to benefit the Friendly House in Phoenix.       Syd also volunteered his time to manage local teams when Lefty O’Doul brought the Tokyo Giants on tour to Tucson in 1953. Then in 1955, he assembled and managed another local all-star team that played against a major league all-star squad that included Hank Aaron, Larry Doby, and others.    

Cohen’s activities, however, were not confined to baseball. When baseball season ended he would referee local games and coach both women and men's basketball teams in El Paso, until 1947.  Between 1953 and 1954, he became a relator and got to show his creative side, posting adds in the El Paso Times that read: 
3 Bedrooms 11/2 Baths
$9500---$1500 Down
3 Bedrooms, Brick Construction nice living room, separate dining room, hardwood floors, double rock garage. DON’T STOP AT FIRST BASE, COME ALL THE WAY TO
HOME PLATE, AND CALL SYD COHEN, Office 2-6593 or Residence 2-3743  

When Major League Commissioner Ford Frick expressed interest in creating a third major league in 1954, Syd wrote him a letter to consider a club in El Paso “any third major league which may be organized in the future.”   

Sometime between 1957 and 1958, Syd took a job working for the Coors Brewing Company as a sales representative, a position that he retained even while volunteering as pitching coach for the Texas Western College, now the University of Texas at El Paso.  

In 1963 the Cohen brothers were brought long to help El Paso Sun Kings to work with players at home games. Syd tutored pitchers while Andy coached first base.     Syd would continue to coach the El Paso minor league team(s) as pitching coach until 1980.  Meanwhile in March Syd was inducted into the El Paso Athletic Hall of Fame.   That same year, Andy and Syd saw the Texas Western College baseball team play its first game.  Andy started up and managed the new baseball program, while Syd served as pitching coach, volunteer positions that the brothers held until 1978.    

After baseball, Cohen was also active in the bowling leagues, being a part of the Red Roster team for over 10 years.  
--- 
On April 9, 1988, Syd Cohen died from cancer at the age of 81. Ray Sanchez said, “I’m not ashamed to say I wept when I learned that Syd had died.  I’m sure many others did the same.”  Following funeral services, Cohen was interred at B’Nai Zion Cemetery in El Paso. To preserve the memory of Syd and brother Andy, El Paso opened Cohen Stadium in June 1990. The edifice served as a local testament until razed in 2019. 

